<id/02/0296/#concept> a <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E55_Type>, skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Copper"@en ;
    skos:broadMatch <http://aims.fao.org/aos/agrovoc/c_1868>, <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300011020>, <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q753>, <http://zbw.eu/stw/descriptor/14299-5>, <customs-1866/metal/#concept> ;
    skos:broader <id/sub/sub_metals_sem_wd_q753/#concept> ;
    skos:definition "is one of the metals most anciently known. It was named from the island of Cyprus, where it was extensively mined and smelted by the Greeks. It has a reddish brown colour inclining to yellow; a faint but nauseous and rather disagreeable taste; and when rubbed between the fingers it imparts a smell somewhat analogous to its taste. Its specific gravity is from 8·8 to 8·9. It is much more malleable than it is ductile; so that far finer leaves may be obtained from it than wire. It melts at the 27th degree of Wedgewood’s pyrometer, and at a higher temperature it evaporates in fumes which tinge the fire of a bluish green. [Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (Project Gutenberg #44562)]"@en ;
    skos:inScheme <id/scheme/#resource> ;
    skos:notation "0296" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Copper"@en ;
